{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/31579-so-did-the-euthanatos-really-screw-the-pooch-or-what","id":31579,"title":"So did the Euthanatos really screw the pooch, or what?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness"],"posts":[{"date":"2013-12-17T04:21:14-08:00","id":31579,"title":"So did the Euthanatos really screw the pooch, or what?","content":"So it seems to me that with the shadowlands being the way they were, that the Euthanatos' sacred duty to the wheel was entirely misguided.<br /> <br /> Reading Wraith it seems that Oblivion was somehow broken, and that rather than being a primordial recycling center, it was instead a black hole, from which nothing ever returned. We see some evidence that oblivion was broken in how harrowings were believed to once be a stage to help the unfortunate wraith to achieve transcendence, but have been corrupted by oblivion. Werewolf lore about how the Wyrm has gone mad is not helping things, even when you consider that the wyrm and oblivion are not the same thing.<br /> <br /> Plus there is Transcendence, which sounds a bit more like what the Euthanatos have in mind when they give someone the Good Death, even though the practice seems like it would result in a lot more spectres instead.<br /> <br /> So what is the Euthanatos' defense to this? Are they just straight up ignorant of what is really going on in the Shadowlands? Is there any evidence/suggestion that the Euthanatos were right and that oblivion really is the best thing?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1078-froggy711","name":"Froggy711","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1078_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"314"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-17T07:31:58-08:00","id":31653,"title":"","content":"There's really three things factoring in to their decisions.<br /> <br /> One, not every dead human produces a Wraith. Many are just going to wink out of existence once the Euthanatos is done with them. Those that do leave something behind can finish their own Karmic cycle. They're no longer being a problem to the Skinlands, which leads into point two.<br /> <br /> Two, mages are supposed to be more concerned with the living world and the people in it, not the lands of the dead or the nature of Oblivion. No matter how skilled a Euthanatos might be in Spirit and Entropy to go delving into the Shadowlands, they will never make it to places like the Labyrinth or Oblivion itself. Oblivion may or may not recycle the souls, but even the most experienced Labyrinth spelunking Wraith doesn't know for certain where they go when they go in there.<br /> <br /> And three, they are also more concerned with the Avatar of the person rather than their own soul. As far as they are concerned, getting the Avatar reincarnated into a new person (who could potentially Awaken) is more important, especially since those who are getting The Good Death seem to be a waste of Karmic space.<br /> <br /> <br /> It's part and parcel of the whole Cosmic Wheel to accept that the choice of The Good Death is not always the most certain and safe, but is considered preferable to letting the person go on damaging the Karmic Cycle.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>- If you must be ridiculous, I must ridicule you.<br /> - Those that can give up essential liberties in exchange for temporary safety deserve neither liberty nor safety. -- Benjamin Franklin</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/375-papa-bear","name":"Papa Bear","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar375_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1920"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-17T10:24:12-08:00","id":31757,"title":"","content":"Even for the Euthanatoi more in-the-know regarding what's going on in the shadowlands (and even assuming you're using Wraith canon as canon in a Mage game) there's the excuse of it being other factions/people's fault for messing things up; not their fault for doing what was supposed to be done on their end of things. Any Tradition mage that knows what a Great Maelstrom is could point their fingers at the Technocrats and lay direct blame on them for the last <i>four</i> of them (the Order of Reason was responsible for the horrific nature of the conquest of the Americas and the Union was responsible for industrialized warfare leading to WWI and WWII, and the Union had a relic nuke and let one of their unhinged operatives get his hands on it and use it on the Labyrinth).<br /> <br /> So on the other side of what Papa Bear just said on their stance of why the Good Death would be used anyway, any of them concerned about the state of the Underworld would likely have the Technocracy as scape goats for why they don't have the time or resources to fix that.<br /> <br /> Also, the Traditions in general, and the Euthanatos vey much in particular, are supposed to be &quot;wrong&quot; from our perspective. However much they construct cohesive philosophies around their beliefs, they are justifying running around murdering people because their faith tells them to. Whether the Good Death was ever, or is now, an actual method of ensuring proper Karmic balance is not supposed to be a canonically answered question. The concept is of a group of people that, despite any actual evidence that what they're doing is making the world a better place, are using their belief that they're making the world a better place by killing people they personally deem unworthy of continued living is supposed to be unsettling.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-17T16:35:39-08:00","id":31984,"title":"","content":"Froggy: If the entire Tradition of Euthanatoi were Necronaughts as an example was giving in the Tradition book, then I would say yeah, they're totally misguided in what they do in regards to the Shadowlands and arranging one way tickets there for everyone they kill. However that's not the case. They have a ritual called the Agama-Te, (or Agama-re depending on how many are going) in which they die themselves and enter the Shadowlands. It's not for a Vacation or a suicide jaunt without the permanence. It's their own version of a Seeking and reality check rolled into one. They go because they have to see and experience just what kind of place they are sending the people they kill to. They have to weigh that knowledge with their compassion every time they kill. Dealing death for a Euthanatos is not meant to be made lightly. They have to know that the soul they send on may continue on suffering in the Shadowlands, but their removal will mean less suffering to the living. If you really want to crossover the systems, you can even go so far as to say that the Euthanatos have some connection with the Ferrymen and their charges are delivered directly to those what would take them on in the other side to their intended afterlives, neatly skipping all this moral speculation. However you cut it, the Euthanatoi are as removed from the Shadowlands as the beat cop is from the prison system. There's the entire process in between and Justice will be subjective to the individual. (And to some the living justice system is just as broken.) Make of that what you will.<br /> <br /> HA: I have to disagree. The majority of the Euthanatos tradition are not using their beliefs to justify Murder (that's what the Akashics have always accused them of). To use their beliefs to justify murder goes against their said beliefs called the Chadona (See Spokes: 6 and 8) and it also courts callousness and Jhor. (if only all religions rewarded faith kills with huge signs of corruption the likes of which everyone could see) Jhor will get Euthanatos (and any other Mage killing with or using too much Entropy magic) noticed by their peers who will then attempt to restore their faith in humanity and compassion for their fellow man and should that faith, they too will be returned to the cycle, effectively sent to the prison they sent so many other souls. (Not to mention Jhored Magi really stand out as &quot;Stinking of the Wyrm&quot; to any and all Werewolves.) Such things are very Powerful incentives to Euthanatoi to exhaust every other avenue of redemption or salvation for the individual before they deal Death to that person.<br /> <br /> I suggest that if you have more concerns that you please re-read both Tradition books again for a better understanding of what Euthanatos are. To say that they just kill is a disservice to the many different kinds of Death Mages there are and what they do for the cycle. Only a handful of factions in the entire Trad are trained killers and assassins. I don't agree that the cannon work is supposed to be painting them as 'wrong' from our perspective, other than the knee jerk reaction that people would have to the first impressions of a group with DEATH as their nick name. 80% of the cannon material explains how misguided the stereotype is. Not that the Akashic Brotherhood didn't have good reason to go to war with the Euthanatoi justifying more bloodshed with their own faith (Irony!), but that schism has largely been settled by Revised.<br /> <br /> On a side note the Agama used to be ritualized initiation rites for all newcomers to the Euthanatoi Tradition upon their Awakening. In modern times so many Awaken through traumas and are not initiated by the rest of the Tradition as they would have been. This can lead to perpetuation of the perceived ignorance individual Euthanatoi may display in regards to what exists on the other side of the Shroud. If an ST is going to overlook that part of the history required for a Euthanatos character when it's introduced to the game, then most certainly they're more susceptible to that corruption of faith in using their beliefs to justify Murder. Still, any ST looking over the books and seeing the Chadona can tell taking the death rite is required as part of the 8th spoke of the Wheel.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imagizer.imageshack.us/v2/125x125q90/19/w20supporterheavymetal1.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imagizer.imageshack.us/v2/125x125q90/31/25ew.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imagizer.imageshack.us/v2/125x125q90/829/8seg.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><a href=\"http://glyphery.webs.com/\" target=\"_blank\"><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://i.imgur.com/Ut0SZ4h.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /></a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1332-darling_rose","name":"Darling_Rose","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1332_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"434"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-18T01:42:01-08:00","id":32229,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Darling_Rose</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/31579-so-did-the-euthanatos-really-screw-the-pooch-or-what?p=31984#post31984\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The majority of the Euthanatos tradition are not using their beliefs to justify Murder (that's what the Akashics have always accused them of).</div> </div> </div> </div>Except you're using their own philosophy to call the killing &quot;not murder.&quot; Just because they have their own lines of what's a justified killing and what's murder by their standards doesn't mean plenty of what they consider justified killing isn't murder by everyone else's standards.<br /> <br /> People that burned witches at the stake didn't think they were murdering anyone. But they had all the same philosophic rationales as the Euthanatoi do.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> To use their beliefs to justify murder goes against their said beliefs called the Chadona (See Spokes: 6 and 8) and it also courts callousness and Jhor. </div> </div> </div>Callousness yes. Jhor only if they use a lot of magic in their killing (and that doesn't even matter if they consider it murder or if they consider it a Good Death anyway). There's no actual direct risk of Jhor if you kill all your targets with mundane means and set that up with a lot of magic. It could land you some nasty Resonance, but Euthies can rack up nasty Resonance through lots of &quot;approved&quot; actions anyway.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> if only all religions rewarded faith kills with huge signs of corruption the likes of which everyone could see </div> </div> </div>Except that's really not how it works. And plenty of real world faiths did consider anyone who kills another - even if sanctioned - as ritually unclean and in need of purification. The writers of the Euthies didn't make that up whole-cloth. Nor does Jhor stem from Euthanatoic philosophic. All the &quot;huge signs of corruption&quot; happen to an mage that does what the Euthies do; regardless of belief. The Euthies just have it come up more frequently because of what their beliefs drive them to do.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I suggest that if you have more concerns that you please re-read both Tradition books again for a better understanding of what Euthanatos are. </div> </div> </div>It seems to me that you're missing what the author of the Revised book often lamented about how fans perceive the Euthanatos; that they have no actual proof that what they're doing is the right thing. Their justifications for killing are based on their philosophy and by an external perspective there is nothing that actually proves them to be more than religious based vigilantes that kill people without any respect to things like law and due process (aka murder).<br /> <br /> They have zero proof that what they do actually protects the Karmic Cycle. They can't prove that even exists. They have zero proof that what they're doing is actually making the world a better place.<br /> <br /> If you want to cite something in the books that actually contradicts that, be my guest. But please don't tell me to go read the book just because you're misreading me.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Only a handful of factions in the entire Trad are trained killers and assassins. </div> </div> </div>Four out of ten, which is almost half (though if you count the Pallottino and Yum Cimil as factions it's kinda iffy on where they sit; the Pallottino often join the Vrati once they get powerful enough and the Yum Cimil don't really a lot of details but would probably have the same sort of division most of the other cultural groups do of ritual faction + combat faction).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I don't agree that the cannon work is supposed to be painting them as 'wrong' from our perspective,... </div> </div> </div>So, you're completely cool with a group of religious fanatics that think their concepts of who deserves to live and who deserves to die trumps secular society, or even common moral codes? You think of a group of people that might roll some dice and decide you need to die because they rolled that your death is necessary is something you'd totally not care about actually existing?<br /> <br /> Yes, the Revised book makes sure to present them as having a complete cohesive philosophy that doesn't dissolve into hypocrisy the second it gets a drop of water on it. You can understand exactly why a mage might become a Euthanatos and hold to their values and beliefs. But to a normal person in the modern world, they are supposed to be scary; not sympathetic. Hell, they're scary to other mages. They take life and death into their hands; knowing how dangerous it is to do so. They think they have the right and the moral authority to do it anyway. And as has been shown repeatedly in the books, hubris catches far too many of them.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-18T09:49:07-08:00","id":32496,"title":"","content":"This is one of those things I both love and hate about Ascension......the philosophical arguments.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>- If you must be ridiculous, I must ridicule you.<br /> - Those that can give up essential liberties in exchange for temporary safety deserve neither liberty nor safety. -- Benjamin Franklin</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/375-papa-bear","name":"Papa Bear","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar375_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1920"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}